-"""Verified generator for recovering a self-dual-code extension column.
+"""Weight-six witnesses in binary self-dual codes of length 36.
 
-Harada and Munemasa, arXiv:1012.5464, Section 2.1, construct a binary
-self-dual code two coordinates at a time.  Their codimension-one subcode has
-generator [G1 | a | a], where G1 generates a shorter self-dual code and a is
-a column over F_2.  This module changes the row basis by an invertible matrix
-L, displays [L G1 | b | b] with b=L a, and asks for the original a.
+This module turns the native objects in Harada--Munemasa, arXiv:1012.5464,
+into a Track-B witness problem.  Each instance is a list of generator matrices
+for binary self-dual [36,18] codes.  Exactly one matrix is a transformed copy
+of the code displayed in Figure 1; its first original generator row is a
+known weight-six word.  Coordinate permutations and invertible changes of
+row basis carry that certificate to the instance.  The other matrices are
+direct sums of self-dual codes whose exact weight enumerators contain no
+weight-six term.
 
-The answer is inverse-generated.  The checker independently substitutes it
-in L a=b over F_2 and never reads the planted answer.  The ordinary recovery
-algorithm is Gaussian elimination; the deliberately structured row-basis
-change gives a much shorter no-tool route if its low-rank invariant is seen.
+The planted change of basis makes the carried word equal to the XOR of all
+18 displayed rows.  Verification does not use that fact or ``inst["answer"]``:
+because every displayed code is self-dual, it checks a proposed support by
+the 18 exact GF(2) orthogonality equations.
 """
 
 from __future__ import annotations
 
