-## Why Track B
+## Why this is Track B
 
-Figure 1 in Section 4 gives a concrete self-dual `[36,18,6]` code
-`G=(I_18,M)`.  Its first generator row has weight six, and the paper's stated
-parameters `(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)=(0,0,12,4)` give exactly 12 weight-six
-words.  The generator carries that row through an invertible basis change and
-a coordinate permutation; decoys are direct sums of matrices
-`[I_m | J_m-I_m]`, whose exact composed weight enumerators have zero
-weight-six coefficient.  This is transformation plus composition, never a
-search for the planted word.
+Section 1 supplies the exact definitions of duality and equivalence.  Section
+2.1 makes generator matrices, row-basis changes, and coordinate permutations
+the classification objects.  Section 4 and Figure 1 give a self-dual
+`[36,18,6]` code explicitly; its first displayed generator row has weight six,
+and the reported `(gamma,delta)=(12,4)` weight enumerator has exactly 12 such
+words.  The generator carries that known row through invertible basis and
+coordinate transformations.  Its decoys are direct sums of self-dual codes
+whose exactly derived enumerators have zero weight-six coefficient.  Selftest
+also enumerates all `2^18` words of each of the four base-code types and checks
+those enumerators.
 
-This is not Track A.  Theorem 1 is a finite classification into 519,492
-equivalence classes, the paper says its calculations used Magma, and fixed
-weight search is efficient here.  Because a self-dual generator is also a
-parity check, the reference algorithm hashes all three-column syndromes and
-looks for two disjoint triples with the same syndrome.  It is
-`O(b * 36^3)` for `b` blocks.  Across eight hard instances it solved 8/8,
-averaged about 0.253 seconds, and needed at most 371,131 counted XOR/collision
-operations.  The planted ansatz scans the XOR of each complete 18-row basis;
-at hard it needs at most 285 vector-XOR/popcount operations.  Individual-row,
-two-row, greedy, random-combination, and lightest-basis outlier probes all
-failed on 8/8 measured seeds.
+This is not a Track-A hardness claim.  A standard meet-in-the-middle algorithm
+forms the syndrome of every triple of columns and looks for two disjoint
+triples with the same syndrome.  It is `O(b * 36^3)` for `b` displayed blocks,
+solved 8/8 hard instances as expected, and used at most 371,131 counted
+XOR/collision operations (mean 0.383 s, maximum 0.780 s in the recorded local
+run).  The compact route is to try the XOR of the complete 18-row basis in each
+block.  Recognizing that ansatz reduces the hard preset to at most 285 exact
+36-bit XOR/weight-test operations.  Theorem 1's complete list of 519,492
+equivalence classes would itself be a finite lookup; this family does not
+misrepresent that classification as a hard search.
 
