+This is a generator for a native problem from Harada and Munemasa,
+[“Classification of self-dual codes of length 36”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.5464).
 The solver receives several binary `18 x 36` generator matrices for self-dual
-codes and must give the support of any weight-six codeword in one matrix.  The
-definition is exactly the binary inner-product/self-duality definition in
-Section 1 of Harada and Munemasa, [*Classification of self-dual codes of length
-36*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.5464).  A proposed support is cheap to check:
-it has six distinct coordinates and has zero GF(2) inner product with every
-generator row.  Since the code is self-dual, those checks are also sufficient
-for membership.
+codes and must give a block number plus the six coordinates supporting a
+weight-six codeword.  `verify()` checks the selected generator's rank and all
+pairwise inner products over GF(2), then checks the proposed support against
+all 18 generator rows.  Since a self-orthogonal 18-space in `GF(2)^36` is its
+own dual, those parity checks decide membership exactly.  The checker neither
+reads the planted answer nor searches for a codeword.
 
-The local construction and verification gates all pass.  The required external
-oracle evidence is **not complete**: the first bare `harden.py` run reached
-OpenRouter but every request returned HTTP 403, `Key limit exceeded (total
-limit)`.  The retained transcript records provider errors, not model failures;
-this result must not be submitted as hardened until the three harness runs are
-repeated with quota.
-
-## Why Track B
+## Why this is Track B
 
-Figure 1 in Section 4 gives a concrete self-dual `[36,18,6]` code
-`G=(I_18,M)`.  Its first generator row has weight six, and the paper's stated
-parameters `(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)=(0,0,12,4)` give exactly 12 weight-six
-words.  The generator carries that row through an invertible basis change and
-a coordinate permutation; decoys are direct sums of matrices
-`[I_m | J_m-I_m]`, whose exact composed weight enumerators have zero
-weight-six coefficient.  This is transformation plus composition, never a
-search for the planted word.
+Section 1 supplies the exact definitions of duality and equivalence.  Section
+2.1 makes generator matrices, row-basis changes, and coordinate permutations
+the classification objects.  Section 4 and Figure 1 give a self-dual
+`[36,18,6]` code explicitly; its first displayed generator row has weight six,
+and the reported `(gamma,delta)=(12,4)` weight enumerator has exactly 12 such
+words.  The generator carries that known row through invertible basis and
+coordinate transformations.  Its decoys are direct sums of self-dual codes
+whose exactly derived enumerators have zero weight-six coefficient.  Selftest
+also enumerates all `2^18` words of each of the four base-code types and checks
+those enumerators.
 
-This is not Track A.  Theorem 1 is a finite classification into 519,492
-equivalence classes, the paper says its calculations used Magma, and fixed
-weight search is efficient here.  Because a self-dual generator is also a
-parity check, the reference algorithm hashes all three-column syndromes and
-looks for two disjoint triples with the same syndrome.  It is
-`O(b * 36^3)` for `b` blocks.  Across eight hard instances it solved 8/8,
-averaged about 0.253 seconds, and needed at most 371,131 counted XOR/collision
-operations.  The planted ansatz scans the XOR of each complete 18-row basis;
-at hard it needs at most 285 vector-XOR/popcount operations.  Individual-row,
-two-row, greedy, random-combination, and lightest-basis outlier probes all
-failed on 8/8 measured seeds.
+This is not a Track-A hardness claim.  A standard meet-in-the-middle algorithm
+forms the syndrome of every triple of columns and looks for two disjoint
+triples with the same syndrome.  It is `O(b * 36^3)` for `b` displayed blocks,
+solved 8/8 hard instances as expected, and used at most 371,131 counted
+XOR/collision operations (mean 0.312 s, maximum 0.843 s in the recorded local
+run).  The compact route is to try the XOR of the complete 18-row basis in each
+block.  Recognizing that ansatz reduces the hard preset to at most 285 exact
+36-bit XOR/weight-test operations.  Theorem 1's complete list of 519,492
+equivalence classes would itself be a finite lookup; this family does not
+misrepresent that classification as a hard search.
 
 ## Worked demo
 
-The demo is deliberately readable: the first displayed row itself has weight
-six.  The complete rendered matrix is:
+The demo uses the untransformed Figure-1 generator, so a person can solve it on
+paper by noticing that row 0 already has weight six.  This is the complete
+rendered instance for `seed=0`:
 
 ```text
+Weight-six word in binary self-dual codes
+
+All arithmetic is over GF(2): addition is XOR (1+1=0). For binary
+vectors u,v of length 36, their inner product is the parity of the
+coordinates where both have a 1. A binary linear code is the set of
+all XORs of the rows of its generator matrix. Its dual consists of
+all vectors orthogonal to every codeword. A code is self-dual when it
+equals its dual.
+
+Below are 1 independent 18-by-36 binary generator
+matrices. In every matrix the 18 rows are independent and mutually
+orthogonal, so they generate a self-dual code of length 36. At least
+one displayed code contains a word of Hamming weight exactly 6.
+
