+# Weight-six words in self-dual length-36 codes
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate | integer tuple: one block and six coordinates |
+| Intended intuition | ansatz — test the XOR of the complete displayed row basis |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+## What this is, and present trust status
+
+The solver receives several binary `18 x 36` generator matrices for self-dual
+codes and must give the support of any weight-six codeword in one matrix.  The
+definition is exactly the binary inner-product/self-duality definition in
+Section 1 of Harada and Munemasa, [*Classification of self-dual codes of length
+36*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.5464).  A proposed support is cheap to check:
+it has six distinct coordinates and has zero GF(2) inner product with every
+generator row.  Since the code is self-dual, those checks are also sufficient
+for membership.
+
+The local construction and verification gates all pass.  The required external
+oracle evidence is **not complete**: the first bare `harden.py` run reached
+OpenRouter but every request returned HTTP 403, `Key limit exceeded (total
+limit)`.  The retained transcript records provider errors, not model failures;
+this result must not be submitted as hardened until the three harness runs are
+repeated with quota.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Figure 1 in Section 4 gives a concrete self-dual `[36,18,6]` code
+`G=(I_18,M)`.  Its first generator row has weight six, and the paper's stated
+parameters `(alpha,beta,gamma,delta)=(0,0,12,4)` give exactly 12 weight-six
+words.  The generator carries that row through an invertible basis change and
+a coordinate permutation; decoys are direct sums of matrices
+`[I_m | J_m-I_m]`, whose exact composed weight enumerators have zero
+weight-six coefficient.  This is transformation plus composition, never a
+search for the planted word.
+
+This is not Track A.  Theorem 1 is a finite classification into 519,492
+equivalence classes, the paper says its calculations used Magma, and fixed
+weight search is efficient here.  Because a self-dual generator is also a
+parity check, the reference algorithm hashes all three-column syndromes and
+looks for two disjoint triples with the same syndrome.  It is
+`O(b * 36^3)` for `b` blocks.  Across eight hard instances it solved 8/8,
+averaged about 0.253 seconds, and needed at most 371,131 counted XOR/collision
+operations.  The planted ansatz scans the XOR of each complete 18-row basis;
+at hard it needs at most 285 vector-XOR/popcount operations.  Individual-row,
+two-row, greedy, random-combination, and lightest-basis outlier probes all
+failed on 8/8 measured seeds.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The demo is deliberately readable: the first displayed row itself has weight
+six.  The complete rendered matrix is:
+
+```text
+BLOCK 0
+00: 100000000000000000010001010000001100
+01: 010000000000000000101101010000001100
+02: 001000000000000000101011100000011000
+03: 000100000000000000011000100000011000
+04: 000010000000000000101001100000000100
+05: 000001000000000000100110010000000100
+06: 000000100000000000111111000100011101
+07: 000000010000000000001111111011011101
+08: 000000001000000000011001001000100011
+09: 000000000100000000011010110111010011
+10: 000000000010000000110011000110110010
+11: 000000000001000000111100111010001110
+12: 000000000000100000000000111001110100
+13: 000000000000010000110000000101110111
+14: 000000000000001000101001111101000010
+15: 000000000000000100010110111110111101
+16: 000000000000000010010101111011111011
+17: 000000000000000001011001110111110100
+```
+
+Its answer is `<answer>0, 0, 19, 23, 25, 32, 33</answer>`.
+`verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`.  Dropping the last
+coordinate returns `(False, "answer must contain exactly seven integers")`.
+A person can solve this demo on paper by counting the ones in row 00; regular
+presets remove that visible low-row shortcut.
+
+## Presets and gates
+
+| preset | length-36 blocks | basis mixing | compact worst case | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 1 | none | visible row | hand example |
+| easy | 8 | 180 paired operations | 144 ops | first oracle rung |
+| medium | 12 | 220 paired operations | 216 ops | available |
+| hard | 15 | 260 paired operations | 285 ops | configured shipping preset |
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 preset-seed plants verified; every block independently checked rank 18 and self-orthogonality |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | tagged answer round-tripped through prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; exact density `12 / 29,216,880 = 4.1072e-7` |
+| G5 | demo brute-force count 12; strongest failing small-basis attack tested 20,520 combinations over eight hard instances |
+| G6 | four attacks 0/8; reference and compact algorithms both 8/8 |
+| G7 | 30-block doubled instance built and verified with the same seven-atom answer |
+| G8 | 80/80 relabellings invariant and carried answers valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 19 characters for the measured answer (22 worst case), 7 atoms (6-token worst case), 285 intended operations |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+The only attempted bare run stopped before a scored attempt.  Four retry
+records were written, all HTTP 403 quota errors; `harden.py` correctly exited
+without a verdict.
+
+| arm | solved / scored attempts | verdict or blocker |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | OpenRouter total key limit; four unscored errors retained |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 | attempted in scratch; OpenRouter total key limit, four unscored errors retained |
+| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | attempted in scratch; OpenRouter total key limit, four unscored errors retained |
+
+Thus `hinted - placebo` is not yet measurable.  The structural hint names only
+the complete-basis parity invariant and does not state its value or give a
+multi-step procedure.  Once quota is restored, run the bare harness in this
+directory, and the hinted/placebo arms in separate scratch directories so the
+bare evidence is not overwritten.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import random
+import gen_1012_5464 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY])
+question = g.render(inst)
+candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + ",".join(map(str, inst["answer"])) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+assert g.search_space(inst) == 15 * 1_947_792
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit only after valid oracle evidence exists:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1012.5464 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The compact route is deliberately basis-dependent: an arbitrary additional
+row operation destroys it, even though it preserves the code.  This benchmark
+therefore measures discovery of a planted full-basis ansatz, not general
+minimum-distance decoding or code equivalence.  The exact random-guess density
+uses a prior uniform over a valid block and a sorted six-subset; it already
+enforces all answer-format constraints but does not model syndrome-guided
+guessing.  The standard triple-collision attack succeeds as expected, so this
+family is unsuitable for Track A.  No external code-equivalence package,
+information-set decoder, or Magma routine was run; the implemented specialist
+attack is sufficient for weight six but not a benchmark of those systems.
+
+`canonical_key` uses the multiset of full block weight enumerators.  It is
+invariant under the tested row, coordinate, and block relabellings and separates
+the generated variants used in G8, but weight enumerators are not complete
+isomorphism invariants, so rare unrelated codes could collide.  Most
+importantly, no four-vendor hardness claim exists until OpenRouter quota is
+restored and all three required arms are run.
